==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|sco|pernickety}}, {{m|sco|persnickety}}, of uncertain origin; the ''Dictionary of the Scots Language'' says that it resembles {{m|sco|per-||intensifying prefix}} + {{m|sco|nick}}, but might be derived from ''particular'' + ''finicky'' with the form influenced by past participles ending in ''-et'', ''-it'', ''-ed''.<ref>{{R:DSL|pernicketie}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pɜː(ɹ)ˈnɪk.ɪ.ti/|/pɜː(ɹ)ˈnɪk.ə.ti/|a=British}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-pernickety.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪkɪti|ɪkəti|s=4}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|UK|Irish|Commonwealth}} [[fussy|Fussy]]; paying [[undue]] [[attention]] to [[minor]] [[detail]]s; [[fastidious]].
#: {{syn|en|niggly|persnickety<q:US>|Thesaurus:fastidious}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1953|author=w:Ian Fleming|page=48|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/casinoroyale00flem/page/48/mode/1up|chapter=8|title={{w|Casino Royale (novel)|Casino Royale}}|passage=‘You must forgive me,’ he said. ‘I take a ridiculous pleasure in what I eat and drink. It comes partly from being a bachelor, but mostly from a habit of taking a lot of trouble over details. It’s very '''pernickety''' and old-maidish really, but then when I’m working I generally have to eat my meals alone and it makes them more interesting when one takes trouble.’}}
# Requiring attention to minor details.

